---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../../components/Ornament.astro";
import Crosswalk from "../../islands/Crosswalk.svelte";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { cuts, voices } from "../../lib/portal-data.js";

const allCuts = [...cuts()].sort((a, b) => a.n - b.n);
const allVoices = [...voices()].sort((a, b) => a.order - b.order);

// Trim the payload to what the island actually renders.
const cutData = allCuts.map((c) => ({
  id: c.id,
  n: c.n,
  title: c.title,
  short: c.short,
  takes: c.takes.map((t) => ({ voice: t.voice, term: t.term, take: t.take, quote_id: t.quote_id ?? null, gap_note: t.gap_note ?? null })),
}));
const voiceData = allVoices.map((v) => ({ id: v.id, name: v.name, short: v.short, branch: v.branch }));

const filled = allCuts.reduce((n, c) => n + c.takes.length, 0);
const possible = allCuts.length * allVoices.length;
const quoted = allCuts.reduce((n, c) => n + c.takes.filter((t) => t.quote_id).length, 0);
---

<Layout
  title="The crosswalk"
  description="Seven throughlines against twelve voices — the whole argument on one grid, including where the material runs out."
  current="/compare"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">The crosswalk</span>
    <h1>Seven motifs, twelve hands</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hand this grid any motif and it will tell you what each voice made it carry. It is the chart
      the whole portal is built on, and its most useful feature is the holes.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      {filled} of {possible} cells filled, {quoted} of them carrying a validated quotation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Crosswalk cuts={cutData} voices={voiceData} base={withBase("/")} client:visible />

  <Ornament />

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <span class="rubric">How to read it</span>
    <h2>Two different kinds of nothing</h2>
    <p>
      Most of this grid is empty, and that matters. An empty cell can mean two quite different
      things, and running them together would misrepresent both the authors and the evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sometimes a voice simply never touches a motif. Gottfried has nothing to say about the Grail;
      he was writing a different book. That is not a defect in the record &mdash; it is a fact about
      what he wrote, and it is worth seeing that only one author, Malory, reaches all seven.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sometimes a voice has a great deal to say and the evidence cannot be published here.
      Chrétien invented the Grail, and the poem in which he did it has no clean public-domain
      English translation; the Vulgate is the hinge of the whole French tradition and its only modern
      translation is in copyright. Those cells are marked as versions without quotations, because
      the honest statement is <em>we know what he says and cannot show you</em>, not silence.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <a href={withBase("/method/whats-not-here/")}>The full account of what is missing &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
